1
00:00:07,350 --> 00:00:10,276
Every front-end developer knows this question:

2
00:00:10,276 --> 00:00:12,757
does the build really match the design?

3
00:00:13,507 --> 00:00:16,407
FrameCheck answers it in five steps.

4
00:00:17,157 --> 00:00:21,624
Three kinds of defect: missing, clipped, misplaced.

5
00:00:24,574 --> 00:00:28,361
Let's try it on a checkout page I designed for a coffee subscription.

6
00:00:30,174 --> 00:00:33,987
I drop in the design export, and a screenshot of the build.

7
00:00:34,737 --> 00:00:37,271
Both must be captured the same way.

8
00:00:37,271 --> 00:00:43,062
FrameCheck reads the image size and suggests the pixel ratio, so the two line up

9
00:00:43,062 --> 00:00:44,220
pixel for pixel.

10
00:00:45,037 --> 00:00:48,885
I pair them, and add a note about what matters on this page.

11
00:00:48,885 --> 00:00:54,206
Notes give the model context, but they can't change its rules or its output format.

12
00:00:59,737 --> 00:01:04,387
Now, one request to Meta's Muse Spark, with both images.

13
00:01:05,137 --> 00:01:08,512
It answers in strict, structured JSON:

14
00:01:08,512 --> 00:01:12,686
a category, a summary, and a box on each image.

15
00:01:13,436 --> 00:01:16,885
Three findings, with the model and token cost.

16
00:01:19,835 --> 00:01:26,299
The promo code row is missing. Both images zoom to the same spot, with a dashed box

17
00:01:26,299 --> 00:01:28,403
where it should be. Accept.

18
00:01:29,153 --> 00:01:34,817
The pay button is narrower than designed, boxed on both images, so the difference is

19
00:01:34,817 --> 00:01:35,761
easy to check.

20
00:01:36,653 --> 00:01:39,461
And the first product title is cut off.

21
00:01:39,461 --> 00:01:44,646
Nothing counts until a person decides, so each one gets a human verdict.

22
00:01:47,596 --> 00:01:52,112
Swipe across the two images to see each change at the same position:

23
00:01:52,112 --> 00:01:56,164
the promo row vanishes, the button shrinks, the title is cut.

24
00:01:58,096 --> 00:02:02,164
Or pull them apart in 3D. The build floats above the design.

25
00:02:02,164 --> 00:02:07,725
Flatten it into an overlay, or turn the stack and follow a finding from the design

26
00:02:07,725 --> 00:02:13,353
straight up to the build. Every box is anchored to the original pixels, so it lines

27
00:02:13,353 --> 00:02:14,370
up at any zoom.

28
00:02:17,396 --> 00:02:22,233
Finally, export the reviewed findings as Markdown for the pull request, with the

29
00:02:22,233 --> 00:02:24,893
model, the tokens, and every human decision.

